 "result": "I created two files in the repository root. I couldn't build or run the image in this session, so neither file has been tested.\n\n- **`Dockerfile`**:\n  - It is a two-stage build on `node:22-bookworm-slim`. The first stage runs `npm ci --omit=dev` and has `python3`, `make` and `g++` as a fallback in case `better-sqlite3` has to compile.\n  - The runtime stage copies in `node_modules` and the sources, runs as the non-root `node` user, and starts the app with `node server/index.js`.\n  - It sets `NODE_ENV=production`, `PORT=3000` and `STORAGE_BACKEND=json`, exposes port 3000, and declares `/app/data` as a volume. A TCP healthcheck on the port also works when SSL is enabled.\n- **`.dockerignore`**:\n  - It keeps `.env*`, `ssl/`, `.git`, `node_modules`, the local SQLite and audit-log files, backups and dev tooling out of the image.\n  - It does not exclude `docs/`, because the server serves it.\n\n**`JWT_SECRET`:** With `NODE_ENV=production`, the server refuses to start unless you pass a `JWT_SECRET` of at least 32 characters at runtime. It also rejects placeholder values such as the one in `.env.docker`. For example:\n\n```\ndocker run -p 3000:3000 -e JWT_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32) -v isms-data:/app/data isms-builder\n```\n\nI left `.env.docker` out of the image so no secrets are baked in. You can pass it with `--env-file` after setting a real secret.\n\n**Data persistence:** User data lives in `/app/data`. A new named volume is filled from the bundled seed data on first use. If you bind-mount a host directory instead, it must be writable by uid 1000 and already contain the seed JSON files.",
